      // Encyclopedia.com's independent trade-reference account documents she insisted on "producing only the highest quality cosmetics," extending to specific packaging-color decisions, and documents the company's institutionalized practice -- confirmed by the same independent source -- that "a final and secret ingredient was always added by a member of the Lauder family" to every batch. TIME's Carol Phillips (a named colleague, founder of the company's Clinique line) independently corroborates an exacting standard applied to her own working environment: "Her conference room was like a dining room, and everything was perfect." The same Encyclopedia.com source documents a harsher edge to the same standard applied to staff: no room in her organization for the "T. and T. salesgirl, always on the telephone or toilet." Two independent sources converging on the same specific pattern -- documented, 71-84 band; dual_edged because the evidence for exacting quality control is inseparable from evidence of an unforgiving standard for employees.
      perfectionism: [82, 0.68, "d", "D"],
      // Encyclopedia.com singles out packaging color/presentation as a specific, attributable instance of her "attention to detail." Leonard Lauder's independently authored memoir (CBS-verified excerpt) documents a distinct specific practice: as a teenager she "kept a careful record of the results" while testing formulas, tracking precisely which ingredients cleared blemishes versus which moisturized -- a recorded, attributable practice, not a general reputation. Two independent sources, each describing a distinct specific instance of the same trait -- documented, 71-84 band.
      detail_orientation: [76, 0.6, "d", "A"],
      // TIME (Grace Mirabella) documents she "stalked the bosses of New York City department stores until she got some counter space at Saks Fifth Avenue in 1948," and that even 40 years into the business she still personally attended every launch of a new counter or shop worldwide, traveling to Moscow and other cities to do so. PBS/WGBH independently corroborates the Saks breakthrough and the immediate sustained personal follow-through required to fulfill the resulting order. Two independent sources on a single sustained, decades-spanning pattern -- documented, 71-84 band.
      persistence: [80, 0.65, "d", "A"],
      // Asked by TIME's reporter for the single word that defines his mother, her son and successor Leonard Lauder answered directly: "Ambition." The same profile documents she "simply outworked everyone else in the cosmetics industry" and continued personally attending product launches worldwide four decades after founding the company. Leonard Lauder's memoir independently documents a compulsive, sustained drive evident from childhood -- persistent enough in giving unsolicited facials to family, friends, and classmates that her father complained, "Esty, stop fiddling with other people's faces." Two independent sources, each with a specific attributable instance spanning her whole life -- documented, 71-84 band, kept at the lower end because the childhood anecdote and the adult work-pattern are related but not identical evidence.
